
  Also worth flagging: [1mthere are no tasks yet.[0m [38;5;153m<main>[39m is empty — no task model, no storage, no add/edit/complete. A
  notifications system needs something to observe. Whatever we design here will either depend on task CRUD being built
  first, or include it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what actually fires a notification, given that the only actor is the person sitting at the browser.

  - [1mTime-based reminders[0m — due dates and "this is overdue now." The clock is the event source. This is the only option
    where a notification tells you something you didn't already know. Needs a scheduler and a due-date field on tasks;
    wants the Notification API for delivery outside the tab.
  - [1mChange feed / activity log[0m — a record of edits, with toasts as they happen. Cheap, no scheduler, but in a
    single-user app it's mostly telling you what you just did. Useful mainly as undo-affordance and history.
  - [1mCross-device sync alerts[0m — "changed on your phone." Genuinely useful, but it contradicts the no-server answer. It
    would need a backend, which reopens the previous question.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m time-based reminders. It's the only one of the three that delivers actual information to a single
  local user, and it's the interpretation that makes "tasks they care about" meaningful — the ones with deadlines. I'd
  pair it with a small [38;5;153mwatched[39m flag so "care about" is explicit rather than inferred.
